<Base title="The Data That Matters"><section class="interior"><div class="eyebrow">WORK / WORKSHOP FRAMEWORK</div><h1>The Data That Matters</h1><p class="lede">Start with business strategy. Measure what matters.</p><p class="lede" style="font-size:16px">A working session for leaders who need a shared definition of success before building another dashboard.</p><div class="callout"><h2>Where we start</h2><p>Developed through dozens of working sessions across multiple organizations, this is an adaptable approach, not a fixed presentation or a prepackaged dashboard.</p><p>We begin with the decisions the business needs to make. Then we work backward to the information, definitions, owners, and systems that support those decisions.</p></div><div class="callout"><h2>Five questions I bring into the room</h2><ol class="work-questions"><li><strong>What are we trying to accomplish?</strong> Which business outcomes matter most over the next 6–12 months?</li><li><strong>What decisions depend on better information?</strong> Who makes those decisions, and how often?</li><li><strong>Which numbers do we trust?</strong> Where do teams use the same words but calculate different things?</li><li><strong>Where does the data come from?</strong> What changes between source, transformation, report, and interpretation?</li><li><strong>What would change if we got this right?</strong> What action could we take that we cannot take confidently today?</li></ol></div><div class="callout"><h2>What the work produces</h2><p>Shared business priorities, a common measurement vocabulary, visibility into conflicting definitions or missing inputs, clearer ownership, and a practical sequence for improving reporting and decision-making.</p><p>The point isn't more data. It's a better conversation about the business, supported by information people can use.</p></div><p><a href="/work/">← Back to Work</a></p></section></Base>
